    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Ernst Mayr - Wikipedia\nauthor: Authority control databases\ndate: 2001-01-30\n---\nname: Ernst Mayr\nbirth_name: Ernst Walter Mayr\nbirth_date: 5 July 1904\nbirth_place: Kempten, Bavaria, German Empire\ndeath_date: 3 February 2005\ndeath_place: Bedford, Massachusetts, U.S.\nnationality: German American\nfield: Systematics, evolutionary biology, ornithology, philosophy of biology\neducation: University of Greifswald, Humboldt University of Berlin\nprizes: * Leidy Award * Darwin-Wallace Medal * Daniel Giraud Elliot Medal * National Medal of Science * Linnean Medal * Balzan Prize * Darwin Medal * International Prize for Biology * Crafoord Prize\nfather: Otto Mayr\nmother: Helene Pusinelli Mayr\nspouse: Margarete \"Gretel\" Simon (m. 1935)\nchildren: 2\n\nErnst Walter Mayr ( ; ; 5 July 1904 \u2013 3 February 2005) was a German-American evolutionary biologist. He was also a renowned taxonomist, tropical explorer, ornithologist, philosopher of biology, and historian of science.Rennie, J. (1994), Profile: Ernst Mayr \u2013 Darwin's Current Bulldog, Scientific American 271 (2), 24\u201325. His work contributed to the conceptual revolution that led to the modern evolutionary synthesis of Mendelian genetics, systematics, and Darwinian evolution, and to the development of the biological species concept.\n\nAlthough Charles Darwin and others posited that multiple species could evolve from a single common ancestor, the mechanism by which this occurred was not understood, creating the species problem. Ernst Mayr approached the problem with a new definition for species. In his book Systematics and the Origin of Species (1942) he wrote that a species is not just a group of morphologically similar individuals, but a group that can breed only among themselves, excluding all others. When populations within a species become isolated by geography, feeding strategy, mate choice, or other means, they may start to differ from other populations through genetic drift and natural selection, and over time may evolve into new species. The most significant and rapid genetic reorganization occurs in extremely small populations that have been isolated (as on islands).\n\nHis theory of peripatric speciation (a more precise form of allopatric speciation which he advanced), based"
